1
00:00:02,919 --> 00:00:06,157
One of those buyers was an
Atlanta businessman named Asa

2
00:00:06,157 --> 00:00:10,756
Candler. By 1891, he
controlled the whole thing.

3
00:00:10,756 --> 00:00:14,694
The total price, by the usual
count: about two thousand three

4
00:00:14,694 --> 00:00:16,195
hundred dollars.

5
00:00:16,195 --> 00:00:17,870
Candler wasn't a chemist.

6
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:21,857
He was a salesman. And he
understood something simple: nobody

7
00:00:21,857 --> 00:00:24,357
buys a drink they've never tasted.

8
00:00:24,357 --> 00:00:25,845
So he gave it away.

9
00:00:25,845 --> 00:00:29,669
The company handed out and mailed
tickets for a free glass.

10
00:00:29,669 --> 00:00:35,582
By its own later estimate, between
1894 and 1913, about one in

11
00:00:35,582 --> 00:00:39,109
nine Americans got a free
Coca-Cola this way.

12
00:00:39,109 --> 00:00:41,484
Then he made sure you
couldn't forget the name.

13
00:00:41,484 --> 00:00:46,122
It went on clocks, calendars,
serving trays, paper fans...

14
00:00:46,122 --> 00:00:49,667
anything that would hang on a
wall or sit on a counter.

15
00:00:49,667 --> 00:00:54,442
By 1895, Coca-Cola was
sold in every state.

16
00:00:54,442 --> 00:00:58,517
And in the early 1900s, the cocaine
was taken out of the coca

17
00:00:58,517 --> 00:00:59,998
leaf extract.

18
00:00:59,998 --> 00:01:03,398
Candler had built a hit at
the soda fountain.

19
00:01:03,398 --> 00:01:06,898
Now he was about to make one of the
strangest deals in business

20
00:01:06,898 --> 00:01:08,240
history.
